 const adviceEn=['Agree how decisions, personal space and responsibilities will be handled instead of trying to redesign each other’s temperament.','Emotional response speed may differ; name the need first and discuss the decision after the intensity settles.','Important matters should be discussed directly and with full context; short messages or third-party explanations can magnify misunderstanding.','Learn the different languages of affection—words, time, acts of help and closeness—rather than measuring love by one style.','Work, money, parents, residence and privacy expectations should be explicit rather than assumed.','Intimacy should grow only through safety, consent, privacy and trust.','Home peace depends on daily agreements around routine, noise, guests, privacy and household work.','Respect both families while keeping clear boundaries around decisions that belong to the couple.','Income, fixed costs, personal spending, savings and major obligations should be transparent within one plan.','Career growth should not become competition; discuss the family impact before major work decisions.','Share responsibilities by time, skill and current circumstances rather than gender or inherited habit.','For major decisions, separate emotional reaction from the practical choice and move in an order both can accept.','Avoid final decisions while angry; use a cooling-off method that returns to the current issue only.','An apology is stronger when it includes the concrete change that prevents repetition.','Trust is built by consistent behaviour around money, communication, boundaries and plans.','Closeness and independence can coexist when personal time, friends and interests are explicitly respected.','Friends should support the relationship; agree before sharing sensitive couple matters outside.','Future parenting values should eventually align around discipline, education, spending and family involvement.','Respect for each other’s thinking matters more than equal qualifications for long-term intellectual companionship.','Shared values should guide difficult decisions rather than become a tool for controlling the other partner.','Relocation should be judged through work, housing, parents, money and support network as one plan.','Small agreements around sleep, food, work hours, phone time and weekly rhythm often create daily peace.','Debt, guarantees, property and family-money obligations should be documented and understood by both.','Long-term stability depends on repairing repeated small patterns before they harden into habits.','D9 can support maturity over time, but boundaries, responsibility and realistic expectations must keep developing.'];
